Chapter: NetPollCalibrated
  cites Net chapter NetDriver
  cites Net chapter NetIO
  cites Kernel chapter Hpet

 NetIO measures time in empty receive polls, and how long a poll takes is a
 property of the driver underneath rather than of NetIO. Measured 2026-08-14,
 one million empty polls cost 15.52 s on the NE2000 model and 0.029 s on the
 e1000: an NE2000 poll is a port IN and therefore a VM exit, an e1000 poll
 reads a descriptor already in RAM. Every bound in NetworkStack is a count of
 ticks, so the fixed 100000 that shipped until 2026-08-14 put the e1000's
 initial SYN retransmit at 8.6 ms and abandoned a live peer after 405 ms,
 while the NE2000 took 219 s to notice a peer had gone.

 The judge is a BAND and not a number because a duration is not reproducible.
 10 ms to 1000 ms excludes both measured failures with margin in each
 direction, and it is what the arm below and the control below it are both
 read against.

Section: Judges

  band-lo-ms : Integer = 10
  band-hi-ms : Integer = 1000

  in-band : Integer -> Text
  in-band (ms) = if ms >= band-lo-ms & ms <= band-hi-ms then "in band" else "out of band"

  spin : Integer -> [Network.Read] Integer
  spin (n) =
   if n <= 0 then 0
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    f <- net-driver-recv-frame
    let r = __heap-restore hp
    in spin (n - 1)
   end

  time-polls : Integer -> [Network.Read] Integer
  time-polls (n) =
   let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in let t0 = hpet-ticks
   in act
    z <- spin n
    let t1 = hpet-ticks
    in (t1 - t0) * 1000 / rate
   end

Section: Entry

 The control is the same judge applied to the interval NetIO used to carry.
 It is what makes the row above a measurement: without it a band this wide
 would report "in band" whatever the calibration did, and the arm could not
 be seen to fail. On the e1000 the old constant is 2.9 ms and on the NE2000
 it is 1.55 s, so the control reads out of band on either card.

  opening : [Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] Nothing = act
    card <- net-driver-bring-up
    let iv = net-io-tick-interval
    in act
      calibrated <- time-polls iv
      fallback <- time-polls net-driver-poll-fallback
      print-line-uni ("card       : " & net-driver-card-name card)
      print-line-uni ("calibrated : " & in-band calibrated)
      print-line-uni ("control    : " & in-band fallback)
      print-line-uni ("fuel clears: " & (if net-io-max-polls > iv * 288 then "yes" else "no"))
    end
  end

